{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013HFR6Y4ZzJC4raeyJzUndA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tCreated-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n2\t# Slice 7a report: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures (opus, engineer)\n3\t\n4\tWorktree `srvm-phase7a`, from `23a8caef`. Nothing committed. JVM only, all runs in the foreground (one quick-tier run of every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace went past the 10-minute foreground limit, finished in the background and was read from its log).\n5\t\n6\t## Verdict\n7\t\n8\t7a is done as the plan's slice section and §3.1, §3.3 and §3.4 describe it, and its own acceptance holds: the two new suites, the ledger fixture, crash cuts, the eight authority tests and the holder tests that use fixtures all pass. `rows_test` and the safepoint harness pass with **no root id moved**.\n9\t\n10\t**7a cannot land alone (design defect D1 below).** The plan's switch of the authority to the rows inspector makes the authority refuse every version-1/2 body. 141 deftests in six old-codec D/E suites now fail. Those suites are re-based in 7b/7c, and I proved the switch is the only cause. I did not work around it, because a fix would change semantics; the decision belongs to the orchestrator.\n11\t\n12\t## Verification\n13\t\n14\t- Lint: `clojure -M:kondo` needed approval in this session, so I used `clj -M:kondo --lint <files>`. Final run over all 22 changed `.clj[c]` files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n15\t- Quick tier, new and touched namespaces plus `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test`, the old `checkpoint_test` and `lift_v1_test` (20 namespaces): **360 tests, 7188 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7a-final.log`).\n16\t  - `rows_checkpoint_test`: 17 deftests.\n17\t  - `rows_custody_test`: 11 deftests, 107 assertions before the M3 fix added two more.\n18\t- Slow tier (`-i :slow`) on the same namespaces plus `rows_test` and the harness: 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures.\n19\t- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier (`-r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`, `target/7a-ucf-quick.log`): 694 tests, 648 failures and 89 errors. They are all in six suites (see D1):\n20\t\n21\t  | Namespace | Failing deftests | Failing assertions |\n22\t  |---|---|---|\n23\t  | `holder.driver-test` | 92 | 294 |\n24\t  | `compose-rows-test` | 35 | 335 |\n25\t  | `compose-test` | 6 | 35 |\n26\t  | `stage-e-rows-test` | 4 (the quick ones) | 35 |\n27\t  | `fencing-rows-test` | 3 | 30 |\n28\t  | `handoff-v2-authority-test` | 1 | 8 |\n29\t\n30\t  No other `yin.vm.ucf` namespace fails.\n31\t- Counterfactual check: I pointed the three authority requires back at `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` and ran `fencing-rows-test`, `handoff-v2-authority-test` and `compose-test`: 21 tests, 332 assertions, 0 failures. I then restored the files (byte-compare verified).\n32\t- Node and Dart were not run, as instructed. The new code follows the portability rules: no `for`, no private var access, catches with `:cljd` first, start-from-`{}` assoc, no EDN strings. The `version-float` fixture relies on Node keeping a `Float64` carrier and on Dart's `integer?` rejecting a double, both checked in the source (`rows/decode` gates on `code/reg?`), but this is unverified on those hosts.\n33\t\n34\t## Files\n35\t\n36\tCreated:\n37\t\n38\t| File | Lines | What |\n39\t|---|---|---|\n40\t| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` | 102 | The rows inspector: `inspect address bytes`, `inspect-pack wire`. Address check, canonical CBOR, `rows/decode`, a `:task` root, then `rows/custody-baseline`; adds `:yin.k/root` |\n41\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 238 | Semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support` (it reuses that namespace's AST helpers, one-slot streams, `serve-table`, header parts and `with-op-id`): `new-machine`, `project` with `source-index`, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-linker`, `parked-installer`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `lift`, `attacher` (counting), `receiver` |\n42\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` | 452 | Five bases lifted under a header; 50 mutations (task edits re-emitted, raw pack edits where the defect is the carrier's own); `render`/`read-file`/`fixture`; editing helpers the authority tests use (`base`, `pack-of`, `edit-task`, `edit-custody`, `waits`, `update-put`, `set-op-id`, `update-child`, `enc`) |\n43\t| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 917 | 55 pins, 3 anchors with full bytes |\n44\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc` | 203 | 17 deftests |\n45\t| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 275 | 11 deftests |\n46\t| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` | 22 | Replaces `ledger-v1.txt` (deleted with `git rm`, so the deletion is staged) |\n47\t\n48\tChanged:\n49\t\n50\t- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (411 lines changed per `git diff --stat`, now 2219 lines):\n51\t  - `pack`: rows sorted by id, used by `emit`, `emit-numbered` and `frame->rows`.\n52\t  - The custody section: `max-exact`, `write-slots`, `custody-baseline`, `pack-address`.\n53\t  - `lift-task :header`: `header-custody` and `protect!`, `:op-seq-exhausted`, and the grammar run before emitting.\n54\t  - `check-custody!` rewritten as the custody stage: grammar, then fork under `:exclusive`, then `accept-grant!` ported verbatim. It runs once after the three per-tree stages and before `attach-streams!`.\n55\t  - `with-custody` in restore: issue numbering, gate, custody state, and the fenced gate.\n56\t- `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`: the require switched to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` under the same alias, so each call site is unchanged; docstrings updated.\n57\t- Fixture bodies only:\n58\t  - Named in the plan: `ledger_fixtures`, `ledger_fixture_test` (docstring), `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test`. `crash_cut_test` needed no edit; it follows `lf/park`.\n59\t  - Also needed (deviation 1): `admission_test`, `input_test`, `reclaim_test`, `holder/evidence_test`, `holder/reader_test`, `holder/writer_test`.\n60\t- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`: one expected value (deviation 2).\n61\t\n62\t## What the code does (for review)\n63\t\n64\t- **Custody slot (root slot 16):**\n65\t  - nil for a fork of any kind (owner Q1).\n66\t  - `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` for blocked or parked exclusive roots.\n67\t  - `{:yin.k/origin}` alone for a halted exclusive root.\n68\t  - nil on every child.\n69\t- **`custody-baseline`** is the version-1 header and op-id grammar of `checkpoint.cljc`, keyed off the slot. On top of that it:\n70\t  - refuses any key outside the five custody keys (`:custody-key`), so lease, epoch and holder cannot enter a body;\n71\t  - refuses a slot that is not a map (`:malformed-custody`);\n72\t  - checks each task's kind and role, that each `:install` pending names an install row, and that every child slot is nil;\n73\t  - refuses any op-id in a fork tree (`:fork-op-id`, closing continuation §11 Q4 for 7a).\n74\t\n75\t  Paths are relative to the root task: `[:custody k]`, `[:frames i :op-id …]`, and `[:installs <name> …]` inside children. The intent digest is BLAKE3 of the canonical `[:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot]`, where `[:row n]` becomes `[:row id]`.\n76\t- **The lift** runs the grammar over the abstract task before emitting, the same contract as the version-1 lift running its inspector over its own bytes. It does not decode its own output, which keeps continuation §7.1 step 9 true. A fork lift of a machine that carries op-ids therefore refuses `:fork-op-id`; before 7a the op-ids travelled verbatim.\n77\t- **The lower's custody stage**, in order: grammar; then a fork under `:exclusive` is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; a halted exclusive root needs no grant and is gated `:ended`; `:fenced` skips the grant and gates `:exporting`, with waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`; otherwise `accept-grant!` runs. The grant's `:checkpoint` must equal `rows/pack-address` of the wire, not the root id; a test pins that the root id is refused as `:not-holder`.\n78\t- **Restore:**\n79\t  - `:yin.k/custody` comes from the evidence, with `:yin.k/next-op-seq` equal to the slot's value exactly.\n80\t  - In an exclusive tree every task numbers its `:put` waits (`:yin.k/issue`, `:yin.k/issued`) and is gated `:running`, as `resume-task*` does.\n81\t- **The two identities:**\n82\t  - The pack address is the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, the content-store key, the grant's checkpoint and the report's result.\n83\t  - The root id is the baseline's `:yin.k/root`.\n84\t  - Tested in `the-pack-address-is-storage-and-the-root-id-is-semantic`: address = BLAKE3 of the bytes; root ≠ address; root = the root row's own hash; rows sorted; re-emitting a decoded pack gives the same bytes. Also tested in `a-pack-variant-of-one-root-shares-its-baseline`.\n85\t\n86\t## Mutation checks\n87\t\n88\tDriver: `target/7a-mut/{mut,run}.py`. Each mutant was applied to a backup-guarded copy, run against `rows-custody-test`, `rows-checkpoint-test`, `authority.grant-test` and `ledger-fixture-test`, then restored. Each restore was byte-compared with a clean copy; all were clean.\n89\t\n90\t| # | Mutant | Caught? | By |\n91\t|---|---|---|---|\n92\t| M1 | Custody header by kind swapped (`header-custody` branches) | yes | `the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind`, every base (they no longer lift), 46 deftests |\n93\t| M2 | A grant check skipped (holder vs binding) | yes | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` (\"another holder\") |\n94\t| M3 | Protection, one direction dropped (an enrolled stream classed otherwise accepted) | **survived at first**; yes after the fix | The op-id presence check that follows masked it on the successor. I added the isolating case (a reader pack with no retained write) to `protection-is-checked-in-both-directions` |\n95\t| M4 | Counter restore off by one | yes | `the-counter-is-restored-exactly`, `an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant`, `install-children-lower-…` |\n96\t| M5 | Fork under `:exclusive` accepted | yes | `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive` |\n97\t| M6 | Custody stage after attach | yes | The zero-attach assertions in 4 deftests (30 assertions) |\n98\t| M7 | Unsorted `emit` | yes | `the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes` (anchors), `the-pack-address-…`, the ledger frames, 14 deftests |\n99\t| M8 | Inspector accepting a mutated row (decode's row-hash check off) | yes | `each-mutation-is-refused-as-it-names` (`row-tampered`) |\n100\t| M9 | Baseline root ignored | yes | 11 deftests, including `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` (`variant-other-root`) and the ledger projection digest |\n101\t| M10 (extra) | The lift skips the grammar | yes | `the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids` |\n102\t\n103\t## Deviations\n104\t\n105\t1. **Six more test files rebased.** `authority/{admission,input,reclaim}_test` and `holder/{evidence,reader,writer}_test` offer fixture bodies through `grant/offer!` and break without the switch. Each change is a three-to-five-line `body` helper.\n106\t2. **One harness assertion changed.** `carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree` expected the phase-6 refusal `[:yin.k/unsupported :custody]` for slot `{:custody :exclusive}`. The plan replaces `check-custody!`, so it now expects `[:yin.k/undecodable :custody-key]`. Nothing else in the harness changed, and no root id moved.\n107\t3. **`:fenced` is minimal.** It skips the grant and protection, gates `:exporting` on every task and moves the waits to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`. `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the exact id counter, the recovery header check and moving parked records into the record are left to 7b (plan §3.2 costs the fenced mode there).\n108\t4. **Refusal kinds and paths are mine:**\n109\t   - New kinds: `:custody-key`, `:malformed-custody`, `:policy` (the old inspector gave a policy mismatch no kind), `:kind`, `:role`, `:child-header`, `:fork-op-id`, `:incomplete-install`.\n110\t   - Paths: `[:custody …]`, `[:frames i …]`, `[:installs <name> …]`.\n111\t   - Carrier-level refusals keep `rows/decode`'s own: a missing tag is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :version` on rows, not `:undecodable :body` as on version 1.\n112\t5. **No op-id-on-variant fixture.** It is structurally impossible on rows: the pending table gives `:next` no op-id slot. I added fixtures the old set lacked: `frame-root`, `row-tampered`, `root-role`, `custody-epoch`, `custody-lease`, `custody-not-map`, `fork-op-id`, `variant-other-root`.\n113\t6. **The grammar lives in `rows.cljc`** (`custody-baseline`). `rows.checkpoint` is a thin wrapper around it, because `lower-task` must run the grammar too and `rows.checkpoint` requires `rows` (a cycle otherwise). Behaviour is what plan §3.3 assigns to the inspector.\n114\t7. **Fixture shape.** `rows_checkpoint_fixtures` takes the `refusal` map a mutation must contain, instead of the old `expected` and `place` pair.\n115\t8. **Accessor alias kept.** The authority still calls the inspector through the alias `checkpoint`.\n116\t\n117\t## Design defects found (separate list)\n118\t\n119\t- **D1. 7a cannot land alone.** The authority switch (plan §2, §4 7a \"Change\") refuses version-1/2 bodies at `offer!`, `report!` and `accepted-baseline`. The holder, compose and Stage E paths still offer such bodies until 7b/7c, so 141 deftests in six suites fail (table above; proven by the counterfactual). The plan's 7a acceptance never mentions them.\n120\t  - Options for the orchestrator:\n121\t    1. Merge 7a only together with 7b and 7c.\n122\t    2. Move the three-line authority switch out of 7a into 7c.\n123\t    3. A temporary version dispatch in the authority. This contradicts \"no version dispatch\" and is not recommended.\n124\t  - `handoff_v2_authority_test` (an old-codec test the plan keeps until phase 8) also breaks permanently under any option. It needs deleting or re-basing, which the plan does not list.\n125\t- **D2. Plan §1.1 row-1 contradicts §3.3.** Row-1 wants a re-emitted id-counter variant with \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline, which this brief requires, such a variant's baseline differs, and the ledger refuses it as `:variant-conflict` (pinned by `grant_test` `variant-other-root`). Under sorted `emit`, the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order. I used that for `variant-equal`, `completion_test` and `inherited_test`.\n126\t  - Stage E row-1 (7d) needs a ruling: either the variant is a conflict, or the root leaves the baseline.\n127\t  - Related: `rows/decode` accepts rows in any order, so \"one root, one pack\" holds for what `emit` makes, not for what a reader accepts. Should decode refuse unsorted packs? I left decode unchanged.\n128\t- **D3. Draft amendments to list (no doc edited):**\n129\t  - transport-tuples §5.5 table: fork is nil in every row (owner Q1), and the slot admits only the five custody keys.\n130\t  - continuation §2.5, §2.6, §7.1 step 8 and §7.2's stage table: custody is not phase-6 nil-only any more; the step-10 refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` is replaced by the grammar's kinds and the grant refusals; the stage runs once for the tree after the three per-tree stages.\n131\t  - continuation §11 Q4: closed for the op-id rules (correlation agreement is still not implemented).\n132\t  - UCF 7.11.1: the inspector citation moves from `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`.\n133\t- **D4. The lift serves streams before custody refusals.** v1 refused `:op-seq-exhausted` before lifting; rows refuses it after the walk has called `serve!`. Harmless to the lower's zero-attach law, but a holder that counts serves will see them (7b's export record).\n134\t\n135\t## What 7b needs from me\n136\t\n137\t- `rows/lift-task` with `{:header h :source-index …}` is `holder.export/prepare` and `encode`'s new path; `h` is the same header shape (`:yin.k/enrolled` stays local).\n138\t- `rows/pack-address wire` is the storage key to store and fence. The grant's `:checkpoint` must be that address.\n139\t- `rows/lower-task recv wire attach! {:grant g :protection p}` is the driver's `lower`; `{:exclusive true}` refuses a fork; `{:fenced true}` is the rehydration entry. Finishing fenced is 7b's: recovery-cell-ids, exact id counter, parked records into the record, the header check against `:yin.k/state`.\n140\t- `rows.checkpoint/inspect` answers the old inspector's keys plus `:yin.k/root`; `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` can read `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/occurrence` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from it.\n141\t- `rows_support` builders for the driver, writer and reader tests, and `rows_checkpoint_fixtures`' editing helpers for packs.\n142\t- D1 decides whether 7b lands together with 7a.\n143\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b306c61e-3078-4fbf-aeb8-718be5db0962","uuid":"2472ee43-f471-4945-bed2-e496a7b735e3","timestamp":"2026-10-13T14:32:51.236Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-srvm-p7c/collab/7a-report.md","content":"Created-Local: 2026-10-13 +07\n# Slice 7a report: custody on rows, the rows inspector, the authority's fixtures (opus, engineer)\n\nWorktree `srvm-phase7a`, from `23a8caef`. Nothing committed. JVM only, all runs in the foreground (one quick-tier run of every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace went past the 10-minute foreground limit, finished in the background and was read from its log).\n\n## Verdict\n\n7a is done as the plan's slice section and §3.1, §3.3 and §3.4 describe it, and its own acceptance holds: the two new suites, the ledger fixture, crash cuts, the eight authority tests and the holder tests that use fixtures all pass. `rows_test` and the safepoint harness pass with **no root id moved**.\n\n**7a cannot land alone (design defect D1 below).** The plan's switch of the authority to the rows inspector makes the authority refuse every version-1/2 body. 141 deftests in six old-codec D/E suites now fail. Those suites are re-based in 7b/7c, and I proved the switch is the only cause. I did not work around it, because a fix would change semantics; the decision belongs to the orchestrator.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Lint: `clojure -M:kondo` needed approval in this session, so I used `clj -M:kondo --lint <files>`. Final run over all 22 changed `.clj[c]` files: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- Quick tier, new and touched namespaces plus `rows_test`, the harness, `layout_test`, the old `checkpoint_test` and `lift_v1_test` (20 namespaces): **360 tests, 7188 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (`target/7a-final.log`).\n  - `rows_checkpoint_test`: 17 deftests.\n  - `rows_custody_test`: 11 deftests, 107 assertions before the M3 fix added two more.\n- Slow tier (`-i :slow`) on the same namespaces plus `rows_test` and the harness: 4 tests, 2724 assertions, 0 failures.\n- Every `yin.vm.ucf.*` namespace, quick tier (`-r 'yin\\.vm\\.ucf\\..*'`, `target/7a-ucf-quick.log`): 694 tests, 648 failures and 89 errors. They are all in six suites (see D1):\n\n  | Namespace | Failing deftests | Failing assertions |\n  |---|---|---|\n  | `holder.driver-test` | 92 | 294 |\n  | `compose-rows-test` | 35 | 335 |\n  | `compose-test` | 6 | 35 |\n  | `stage-e-rows-test` | 4 (the quick ones) | 35 |\n  | `fencing-rows-test` | 3 | 30 |\n  | `handoff-v2-authority-test` | 1 | 8 |\n\n  No other `yin.vm.ucf` namespace fails.\n- Counterfactual check: I pointed the three authority requires back at `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` and ran `fencing-rows-test`, `handoff-v2-authority-test` and `compose-test`: 21 tests, 332 assertions, 0 failures. I then restored the files (byte-compare verified).\n- Node and Dart were not run, as instructed. The new code follows the portability rules: no `for`, no private var access, catches with `:cljd` first, start-from-`{}` assoc, no EDN strings. The `version-float` fixture relies on Node keeping a `Float64` carrier and on Dart's `integer?` rejecting a double, both checked in the source (`rows/decode` gates on `code/reg?`), but this is unverified on those hosts.\n\n## Files\n\nCreated:\n\n| File | Lines | What |\n|---|---|---|\n| `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc` | 102 | The rows inspector: `inspect address bytes`, `inspect-pack wire`. Address check, canonical CBOR, `rows/decode`, a `:task` root, then `rows/custody-baseline`; adds `:yin.k/root` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_support.cljc` | 238 | Semantic-register machine builders mirroring `lift_support` (it reuses that namespace's AST helpers, one-slot streams, `serve-table`, header parts and `with-op-id`): `new-machine`, `project` with `source-index`, `parked-reader`, `parked-writer`, `parked-explicit`, `parked-linker`, `parked-installer`, `halted-machine`, `halted-with-cursors`, `parked-module-store-cursors`, `lift`, `attacher` (counting), `receiver` |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_fixtures.cljc` | 452 | Five bases lifted under a header; 50 mutations (task edits re-emitted, raw pack edits where the defect is the carrier's own); `render`/`read-file`/`fixture`; editing helpers the authority tests use (`base`, `pack-of`, `edit-task`, `edit-custody`, `waits`, `update-put`, `set-op-id`, `update-child`, `enc`) |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/rows-checkpoint-v3.txt` | 917 | 55 pins, 3 anchors with full bytes |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_checkpoint_test.cljc` | 203 | 17 deftests |\n| `test/yin/vm/ucf/rows_custody_test.cljc` | 275 | 11 deftests |\n| `test/resources/yin/vm/ucf/ledger-v2.txt` | 22 | Replaces `ledger-v1.txt` (deleted with `git rm`, so the deletion is staged) |\n\nChanged:\n\n- `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows.cljc` (411 lines changed per `git diff --stat`, now 2219 lines):\n  - `pack`: rows sorted by id, used by `emit`, `emit-numbered` and `frame->rows`.\n  - The custody section: `max-exact`, `write-slots`, `custody-baseline`, `pack-address`.\n  - `lift-task :header`: `header-custody` and `protect!`, `:op-seq-exhausted`, and the grammar run before emitting.\n  - `check-custody!` rewritten as the custody stage: grammar, then fork under `:exclusive`, then `accept-grant!` ported verbatim. It runs once after the three per-tree stages and before `attach-streams!`.\n  - `with-custody` in restore: issue numbering, gate, custody state, and the fenced gate.\n- `authority/{grant,completion,admission}.cljc`: the require switched to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint` under the same alias, so each call site is unchanged; docstrings updated.\n- Fixture bodies only:\n  - Named in the plan: `ledger_fixtures`, `ledger_fixture_test` (docstring), `grant_test`, `completion_test`, `inherited_test`, `front_test`. `crash_cut_test` needed no edit; it follows `lf/park`.\n  - Also needed (deviation 1): `admission_test`, `input_test`, `reclaim_test`, `holder/evidence_test`, `holder/reader_test`, `holder/writer_test`.\n- `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/safepoint_harness_test.cljc`: one expected value (deviation 2).\n\n## What the code does (for review)\n\n- **Custody slot (root slot 16):**\n  - nil for a fork of any kind (owner Q1).\n  - `{:yin.k/policy :yin.k/exclusive :yin.k/occurrence :yin.k/arbitration :yin.k/next-op-seq}` plus `:yin.k/origin` for blocked or parked exclusive roots.\n  - `{:yin.k/origin}` alone for a halted exclusive root.\n  - nil on every child.\n- **`custody-baseline`** is the version-1 header and op-id grammar of `checkpoint.cljc`, keyed off the slot. On top of that it:\n  - refuses any key outside the five custody keys (`:custody-key`), so lease, epoch and holder cannot enter a body;\n  - refuses a slot that is not a map (`:malformed-custody`);\n  - checks each task's kind and role, that each `:install` pending names an install row, and that every child slot is nil;\n  - refuses any op-id in a fork tree (`:fork-op-id`, closing continuation §11 Q4 for 7a).\n\n  Paths are relative to the root task: `[:custody k]`, `[:frames i :op-id …]`, and `[:installs <name> …]` inside children. The intent digest is BLAKE3 of the canonical `[:yin.k/append target-identity payload-slot]`, where `[:row n]` becomes `[:row id]`.\n- **The lift** runs the grammar over the abstract task before emitting, the same contract as the version-1 lift running its inspector over its own bytes. It does not decode its own output, which keeps continuation §7.1 step 9 true. A fork lift of a machine that carries op-ids therefore refuses `:fork-op-id`; before 7a the op-ids travelled verbatim.\n- **The lower's custody stage**, in order: grammar; then a fork under `:exclusive` is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`; a halted exclusive root needs no grant and is gated `:ended`; `:fenced` skips the grant and gates `:exporting`, with waits moved to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`; otherwise `accept-grant!` runs. The grant's `:checkpoint` must equal `rows/pack-address` of the wire, not the root id; a test pins that the root id is refused as `:not-holder`.\n- **Restore:**\n  - `:yin.k/custody` comes from the evidence, with `:yin.k/next-op-seq` equal to the slot's value exactly.\n  - In an exclusive tree every task numbers its `:put` waits (`:yin.k/issue`, `:yin.k/issued`) and is gated `:running`, as `resume-task*` does.\n- **The two identities:**\n  - The pack address is the ledger's `:yin.k/id`, the content-store key, the grant's checkpoint and the report's result.\n  - The root id is the baseline's `:yin.k/root`.\n  - Tested in `the-pack-address-is-storage-and-the-root-id-is-semantic`: address = BLAKE3 of the bytes; root ≠ address; root = the root row's own hash; rows sorted; re-emitting a decoded pack gives the same bytes. Also tested in `a-pack-variant-of-one-root-shares-its-baseline`.\n\n## Mutation checks\n\nDriver: `target/7a-mut/{mut,run}.py`. Each mutant was applied to a backup-guarded copy, run against `rows-custody-test`, `rows-checkpoint-test`, `authority.grant-test` and `ledger-fixture-test`, then restored. Each restore was byte-compared with a clean copy; all were clean.\n\n| # | Mutant | Caught? | By |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| M1 | Custody header by kind swapped (`header-custody` branches) | yes | `the-lift-writes-the-custody-slot-by-kind`, every base (they no longer lift), 46 deftests |\n| M2 | A grant check skipped (holder vs binding) | yes | `every-grant-check-refuses-before-any-attachment` (\"another holder\") |\n| M3 | Protection, one direction dropped (an enrolled stream classed otherwise accepted) | **survived at first**; yes after the fix | The op-id presence check that follows masked it on the successor. I added the isolating case (a reader pack with no retained write) to `protection-is-checked-in-both-directions` |\n| M4 | Counter restore off by one | yes | `the-counter-is-restored-exactly`, `an-exclusive-root-lowers-under-its-grant`, `install-children-lower-…` |\n| M5 | Fork under `:exclusive` accepted | yes | `a-fork-is-refused-under-exclusive` |\n| M6 | Custody stage after attach | yes | The zero-attach assertions in 4 deftests (30 assertions) |\n| M7 | Unsorted `emit` | yes | `the-file-holds-exactly-the-declared-bytes` (anchors), `the-pack-address-…`, the ledger frames, 14 deftests |\n| M8 | Inspector accepting a mutated row (decode's row-hash check off) | yes | `each-mutation-is-refused-as-it-names` (`row-tampered`) |\n| M9 | Baseline root ignored | yes | 11 deftests, including `a-conflicting-variant-is-refused` (`variant-other-root`) and the ledger projection digest |\n| M10 (extra) | The lift skips the grammar | yes | `the-lift-refuses-what-custody-forbids` |\n\n## Deviations\n\n1. **Six more test files rebased.** `authority/{admission,input,reclaim}_test` and `holder/{evidence,reader,writer}_test` offer fixture bodies through `grant/offer!` and break without the switch. Each change is a three-to-five-line `body` helper.\n2. **One harness assertion changed.** `carrier-steps-run-in-order-over-the-tree` expected the phase-6 refusal `[:yin.k/unsupported :custody]` for slot `{:custody :exclusive}`. The plan replaces `check-custody!`, so it now expects `[:yin.k/undecodable :custody-key]`. Nothing else in the harness changed, and no root id moved.\n3. **`:fenced` is minimal.** It skips the grant and protection, gates `:exporting` on every task and moves the waits to `:yin.k/recovery-waits`. `:yin.k/recovery-cell-ids`, the exact id counter, the recovery header check and moving parked records into the record are left to 7b (plan §3.2 costs the fenced mode there).\n4. **Refusal kinds and paths are mine:**\n   - New kinds: `:custody-key`, `:malformed-custody`, `:policy` (the old inspector gave a policy mismatch no kind), `:kind`, `:role`, `:child-header`, `:fork-op-id`, `:incomplete-install`.\n   - Paths: `[:custody …]`, `[:frames i …]`, `[:installs <name> …]`.\n   - Carrier-level refusals keep `rows/decode`'s own: a missing tag is `:yin.k/profile-mismatch :version` on rows, not `:undecodable :body` as on version 1.\n5. **No op-id-on-variant fixture.** It is structurally impossible on rows: the pending table gives `:next` no op-id slot. I added fixtures the old set lacked: `frame-root`, `row-tampered`, `root-role`, `custody-epoch`, `custody-lease`, `custody-not-map`, `fork-op-id`, `variant-other-root`.\n6. **The grammar lives in `rows.cljc`** (`custody-baseline`). `rows.checkpoint` is a thin wrapper around it, because `lower-task` must run the grammar too and `rows.checkpoint` requires `rows` (a cycle otherwise). Behaviour is what plan §3.3 assigns to the inspector.\n7. **Fixture shape.** `rows_checkpoint_fixtures` takes the `refusal` map a mutation must contain, instead of the old `expected` and `place` pair.\n8. **Accessor alias kept.** The authority still calls the inspector through the alias `checkpoint`.\n\n## Design defects found (separate list)\n\n- **D1. 7a cannot land alone.** The authority switch (plan §2, §4 7a \"Change\") refuses version-1/2 bodies at `offer!`, `report!` and `accepted-baseline`. The holder, compose and Stage E paths still offer such bodies until 7b/7c, so 141 deftests in six suites fail (table above; proven by the counterfactual). The plan's 7a acceptance never mentions them.\n  - Options for the orchestrator:\n    1. Merge 7a only together with 7b and 7c.\n    2. Move the three-line authority switch out of 7a into 7c.\n    3. A temporary version dispatch in the authority. This contradicts \"no version dispatch\" and is not recommended.\n  - `handoff_v2_authority_test` (an old-codec test the plan keeps until phase 8) also breaks permanently under any option. It needs deleting or re-basing, which the plan does not list.\n- **D2. Plan §1.1 row-1 contradicts §3.3.** Row-1 wants a re-emitted id-counter variant with \"a new root id and new pack; equal baseline\". With `:yin.k/root` in the baseline, which this brief requires, such a variant's baseline differs, and the ledger refuses it as `:variant-conflict` (pinned by `grant_test` `variant-other-root`). Under sorted `emit`, the only equal-baseline variant is the same root in another row order. I used that for `variant-equal`, `completion_test` and `inherited_test`.\n  - Stage E row-1 (7d) needs a ruling: either the variant is a conflict, or the root leaves the baseline.\n  - Related: `rows/decode` accepts rows in any order, so \"one root, one pack\" holds for what `emit` makes, not for what a reader accepts. Should decode refuse unsorted packs? I left decode unchanged.\n- **D3. Draft amendments to list (no doc edited):**\n  - transport-tuples §5.5 table: fork is nil in every row (owner Q1), and the slot admits only the five custody keys.\n  - continuation §2.5, §2.6, §7.1 step 8 and §7.2's stage table: custody is not phase-6 nil-only any more; the step-10 refusal `:yin.k/unsupported :custody` is replaced by the grammar's kinds and the grant refusals; the stage runs once for the tree after the three per-tree stages.\n  - continuation §11 Q4: closed for the op-id rules (correlation agreement is still not implemented).\n  - UCF 7.11.1: the inspector citation moves from `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint` to `yin.vm.ucf.rows.checkpoint`.\n- **D4. The lift serves streams before custody refusals.** v1 refused `:op-seq-exhausted` before lifting; rows refuses it after the walk has called `serve!`. Harmless to the lower's zero-attach law, but a holder that counts serves will see them (7b's export record).\n\n## What 7b needs from me\n\n- `rows/lift-task` with `{:header h :source-index …}` is `holder.export/prepare` and `encode`'s new path; `h` is the same header shape (`:yin.k/enrolled` stays local).\n- `rows/pack-address wire` is the storage key to store and fence. The grant's `:checkpoint` must be that address.\n- `rows/lower-task recv wire attach! {:grant g :protection p}` is the driver's `lower`; `{:exclusive true}` refuses a fork; `{:fenced true}` is the rehydration entry. Finishing fenced is 7b's: recovery-cell-ids, exact id counter, parked records into the record, the header check against `:yin.k/state`.\n- `rows.checkpoint/inspect` answers the old inspector's keys plus `:yin.k/root`; `reopen-holder` and `rebuilt-cell` can read `:yin.k/kind`, `:yin.k/occurrence` and `:yin.k/next-op-seq` from it.\n- `rows_support` builders for the driver, writer and reader tests, and `rows_checkpoint_fixtures`' editing helpers for packs.\n- D1 decides whether 7b lands together with 7a.\n","numLines":143,"startLine":1,"totalLines":143}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_013HFR6Y4ZzJC4raeyJzUndA","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
